title Two rulings the loop left
prompt Keaton, on waking: we can continue incense interactivity. The overnight loop ran six laps and closed on check-in (Claude) with two unruled design questions.
rota none -- an interactive sitting answering two questions the loop left addressed to a ruling; the loop read its own rows overnight.
think Both items were shapes rather than laps, which is exactly what a check-in is for. I took them in order and measured before ruling on either.
obs THE FIRST RULING IS A REFUSAL, AND MEASUREMENT IS WHY. The card counts a bold run closing on a colon as a label, not prose, but only when the colon sits INSIDE the bold -- so `**Label** (aside):` is still counted, 44 lines of 276 on the page that raised it. The obvious repair allows a colon after the bold run with a parenthetical between. Tree-wide that newly excludes 257 lines.
obs THOSE 257 ARE TWO THINGS WEARING ONE SHAPE, AND NO POSITIONAL RULE TELLS THEM APART. 172 are labels -- a noun phrase, a qualifier, a colon, values. 85 are CLAIMS: bold assertion, colon, evidence, which is this tree's own house sentence form. Splitting by bold-run length misfiles the 85; splitting by what follows the colon misfiles as many. The difference is whether the bold run holds a verb, and that is semantics rather than shape.
obs SO THE RULE STAYS, AND THE DECIDING ARGUMENT IS THAT THE ERROR IS ASYMMETRIC. The reading counts NEGATIVE sentences. Counting a label dilutes the ratio slightly toward positive, since a label carries little negation; excluding a claim HIDES real negation, which is the one thing the reading exists to see. Erring toward counting errs in the safe direction. The residue is a known dilution rather than a defect, and the measurement now sits beside the rule so the next reader meets it instead of repeating it.
obs THE SECOND ITEM WAS NOT A MISSING TOOL, IT WAS A TOOL NOBODY COULD FIND. reds_fold_reanchor.sh is a stdin-to-stdout filter that rewrites relative links for prose moving from construction/ down into construction/archive/. Nothing in it is REDS-specific except its NAME. An ITINERARY landed-accounts shelf makes exactly that move, by hand, on every send, with no tool in its path.
obs WHICH IS WHY THE DEPTH FAULT FIRED FIVE TIMES IN ONE WEEK FROM FIVE HANDS. The prevention was reachable and unknown. Both tools now name each other: the filter says it is general and shows the account-shelf invocation, and the repointer I built says prevention lives one directory over. A repair tool that exists because a prevention tool was unfindable should say so, or the next hand builds a third.
obs I BROKE THE CARD WHILE RULING ON IT, AND THE SHELL CAUGHT ME. My first comment went inside a single-quoted awk block carrying apostrophes -- "amphora's", "tree's" -- which closed the quote and broke the file at a line 350 further down. sh -n named it instantly. A comment is program text, and prose habits do not survive contact with quoting.
loom residue_ruling newly_excluded=257 labels=172 claims=85 verdict=rule_stays reason=asymmetric_error
loom account_shelf tool_existed=yes general=yes named_for_reds=yes firings_from_unfindability=5
file tools/fixtures/q/qa_report_card.sh the residue ruled on, with the 257/172/85 split beside the rule
file tools/fixtures/r/reds_fold_reanchor.sh named as general, with the account-shelf invocation shown
file tools/f/fold_shelf_link_repoint.sh points at the prevention it was built to repair after
scope two unruled questions the loop left, both answered by measuring rather than by preference
status GREEN -- qa_report_card witness 149 behaviors, reds_fold and fold_shelf_link_repoint green
recommend keep-going -- the loop closed honestly on both; nothing here needs Keaton's word, and the fleet is turning while incense sits with a hand.
